.TH ATLAS-SQLITE3 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-sqlite3 \- Open a SQLite database file and run SQL on it: list tables, read rows, search, count.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
sqlite3  \-readonly  evidence.db  '.tables'
   |        |            |             '\-\-\- a dot\-command or SQL statement to run, then exit
   |        |            '\-\-\- the database file
   |        '\-\-\- never modify evidence
   '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Browser history, app data and many forensics challenges ship as \fB.db\fR/\fB.sqlite\fR files.
.IP \(bu 2
Practice basic SQL offline.
.IP \(bu 2
Check what an application stores.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\&.tables\fR
List tables.
.br
Use it when: First step.
.TP
\fB\&.schema [table]\fR
Show table definitions.
.br
Use it when: Column names and types.
.TP
\fB\&.headers on / .mode column\fR
Nicer output.
.br
Use it when: Readable tables.
.TP
\fB\&.mode csv / .output FILE\fR
Export.
.br
Use it when: Hand off data.
.TP
\fB\-readonly\fR
Open without write access.
.br
Use it when: Evidence.
.TP
\fBSELECT ... WHERE ... LIKE '%x%'\fR
Search rows.
.br
Use it when: Find a user, URL or message.
.TP
\fBORDER BY / LIMIT / GROUP BY / COUNT(*)\fR
Sort, cut, summarise.
.br
Use it when: Top N, counts per value.
.TP
\fB\&.dump\fR
Print the whole database as SQL.
.br
Use it when: Search it with grep.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Create a small database"
One command creates the file, the table and rows; then \fB.tables\fR lists it.
.PP
.nf
$ rm \-f t.db; sqlite3 t.db "CREATE TABLE users(id INTEGER, name TEXT, role TEXT); INSERT INTO users VALUES (1,'alice','user'),(2,'bob','admin'),(3,'carol','user');" && sqlite3 t.db '.tables'
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) users
.fi
.PP
A database is just a file. Evidence files are opened the same way.
.SS "2. Read it nicely"
\fB\-header \-column\fR formats output as a table.
.PP
.nf
$ sqlite3 \-header \-column t.db 'SELECT * FROM users;'
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) A header row id name role, a dashed line, then three rows.
.fi
.PP
Same result as \fBSELECT * FROM users;\fR in MySQL.
.SS "3. Filter and count"
\fBGROUP BY\fR counts rows per value.
.PP
.nf
$ sqlite3 \-header \-column t.db "SELECT role, COUNT(*) AS n FROM users GROUP BY role;"
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) admin 1 and user 2.
.fi
.PP
Summaries are how you find the odd one out in thousands of rows.
.SS "4. Look at the structure"
\fB.schema\fR prints the SQL that created the table.
.PP
.nf
$ sqlite3 t.db '.schema users'
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) CREATE TABLE users(id INTEGER, name TEXT, role TEXT);
.fi
.PP
MySQL's \fBDESCRIBE\fR equivalent.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Dot\-commands (\fB.tables\fR) are sqlite3 features, not SQL; they take no semicolon.
.IP \(bu 2
Open evidence with \fB\-readonly\fR or a copy.
.IP \(bu 2
SQLite types are loose: text may hide in number columns.
.IP \(bu 2
Browser databases may be locked while the browser runs: copy the file first.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
List the tables of a browser history database copy.
.IP 2. 3
Find the most visited domain with \fBGROUP BY\fR.
.IP 3. 3
Export a table to CSV.
.SH SEE ALSO
MySQL client, DB Browser for SQLite, python sqlite3 module, strings, grep.
